The provided code is a C# class named `ChromaMemoryStore` that implements the `IMemoryStore` interface. It provides methods for creating, deleting, and querying collections, as well as getting and removing memory records.

To write unit tests for this class, we can use the xUnit testing framework. We'll create a test class named `ChromaMemoryStoreTests` and add test methods to it.

Here's an example of how we can test the `DeleteCollectionAsync` method:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Connectors.Chroma;
using Moq;
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Xunit;

namespace Tests
{
    public class ChromaMemoryStoreTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public async Task DeleteCollectionAsync_LogsError_WhenCollectionDoesNotExist()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<ChromaMemoryStore>>();
            var chromaClientMock = new Mock<IChromaClient>();
            var chromaMemoryStore = new ChromaMemoryStore(chromaClientMock.Object, loggerMock.Object);

            chromaClientMock
                .Setup(c => c.DeleteCollectionAsync(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<CancellationToken>()))
                .Throws(new HttpOperationException("Collection does not exist"));

            // Act
            await Assert.ThrowsAsync<KernelException>(() => chromaMemoryStore.DeleteCollectionAsync("non-existent-collection", CancellationToken.None));

            // Assert
            loggerMock
                .Verify(l => l.LogError("Cannot delete non-existent collection {0}", "non-existent-collection"), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```

In this test, we're using Moq to create mock implementations of the `ILogger<ChromaMemoryStore>` and `IChromaClient` interfaces. We're then setting up the `IChromaClient` mock to throw an `HttpOperationException` when `DeleteCollectionAsync` is called.

We're then calling the `DeleteCollectionAsync` method on the `ChromaMemoryStore` instance and asserting that it throws a `KernelException`. Finally, we're verifying that the `LogError` method is called on the `ILogger<ChromaMemoryStore>` instance with the expected message.

Note that this is just one example of how we can test the `ChromaMemoryStore` class. We'll need to add more test methods to cover all the scenarios and edge cases.

